  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 117 PM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nLatest model data continues to support the next Pacific system splitting \nas it moves across the Intermountain West and into the High Plains \nthrough Thursday night. This leaves the region in generally dry west \nto northwest flow through Thanksgiving night. This will also keep \ntemperatures at and above seasonal levels through the period. \n\nIncreasing west to northwest flow remains expected across the region \nFriday ahead of more Eastern Pacific energy moving onshore across the \nPacific Northwest. Breezy northwest winds will help boost temperatures\nwell above seasonal levels into the 60s across the plains on Friday, \nwith highs in the 40s and 50s across the higher terrain. This will \nalso lead to heightened fire danger across the region on Friday. \n \nFor Saturday and Sunday, operational and ensemble model data continue\nto differ on the amplitude of said Pacific Northwest energy, with the \nlatest EC and Canadian data being the further west digging the system \nacross the Rockies on Saturday, which brings better chances of \nprecipitation to eastern Colorado. Latest GFS data keeps the system \nfurther east, keeping precipitation mainly confined to the Northern and \nCentral Mtns. NBM data seemingly splits the difference and has good\nchances of generally light snow across the Central Mountains on \nSaturday, as well as introduced slight chances of precipitation across \nthe plains with a passing cold front. Temperatures look to remain above \nseasonal levels on Saturday, with dry weather and cooler temperatures \nin store areawide behind the passing system on Sunday.  \n\nFor next week, models continue to differ on timing and location of \nthe expected wave train of Eastern Pacific systems translating across\nthe CONUS, with NBM keeping main chances of precipitation along \nand west of the ContDvd, along with temperatures at to slightly \nabove seasonal levels, with day to day changes likely, as said\nwaves become more resolved.",
